tensions are climbing between russia and  the west over concerns moscow is  planning to invade ukraine soon over the  weekend the u.s ordered family members  at its embassy in kiev to leave the  country russian president vladimir putin  denies that he's planning an invasion  but ukraine's ambassador to the uk tells  bloomberg otherwise  we don't believe what  russian said putin is saying just not  because he's on his own territory which  he's claiming but problem is that we're  missing the point that he's been in  ukraine and was fighting with us for  last seven years we're not going all the  way into history but this seven years  already costed us more than thirteen  thousand people dead and seven percent  of territory so why would we believe him  right now  the new york times is reporting  president biden is now considering  deploying troops to eastern europe and  the baltics and sending warships and  aircraft to nato allies so let's get an  on-the-ground perspective from peter  dickinson a ukraine editor at the  atlantic council peter is a british  national who spent more than two decades  living in ukraine and he joins us now  from kiev peter i want to start with the  most recent reports that the white house  may now send thousands of u.s troops uh  to nato allies in anticipation that  russia will move forward with some kind  of invasion that would be a major major  pivot from biden's  previous stance what does that potential  shifted strategy signal to you  well it would be a significant strategy  shift because ukraine i'm sorry nato has  always despite russia's protestations  and and complaints  uh nato has always been very careful  about the amount of troops that it does  station in countries uh close to russia  bordering on russia and in the region in  central and eastern europe so it would  be a major step in uh towards uh a much  stronger native nato presence in the  region which of course is exactly what  uh vladimir putin says he wants to avoid  so there's a certain amount of irony  that his actions may be provoking uh the  the outcome that he most sought to uh to  prevent  um but even so i would i would uh  caution uh reading too much into it  because the numbers are still quite  small you have to bear in mind that  russia at the moment has a master force  close to ukraine  around the country of approximately 100  to 150 000 men uh you know so a few  thousand extra american troops in  central and eastern europe are well  welcomed certainly for the nato  countries in question and also from a  ukrainian point of view i'm not going to  tip the balance that's a good point but  going back to that  idea that there are about 150 100 000  u.s or i should say russian troops in  ukraine along the border that sounds  like a lot but is it enough to  accomplish uh a land invasion if that's  what putin is planning  well that's a good question and the  short answer is no probably not at this  stage ukraine is a huge country it's the  largest country wholly in europe it's a  massive land mass  so talk of a so-called full-scale  invasion and an attempt essentially by  russia to fully occupy the country  military military analysts  are not in total agreement but the  general figure you hear bandied about is  is between maybe three and five hundred  thousand troops would be necessary for  such an operation uh i'd be inclined to  put it towards the higher end of that  scale towards half a million now russia  has nothing like that many troops in in  play uh it would even be a question  whether russia could muster that many  troops i mean they have such soldiers  they have they have more than a million  men technically but russia is also a you  know vast country they need to they need  to secure  the whole the whole of the country they  can't deploy the  their entire military in ukraine as it  were so  uh the force they have at the moment  perhaps is looking for  slightly more limited objectives but  there are many they could do there are  many different uh uh slightly less  ambitious operations that they could  accomplish with this force such as what  peter  um  one would be a land bridge between the  area that's occupied unofficially by  russia and east ukraine and the crimean  peninsula essentially these two two  positions that russia already holds in  ukraine to connect them via a land  bridge  they could look to take over other parts  of southern ukraine they could look to  have an incursion another point on the  border perhaps uh hardkey for example is  ukraine's second largest city it's only  a stone's throw from the russian border  in the east um of course taking a city  is a city of around one and a half  million people so that would be a huge  undertaking again but these are all  options on the table uh that russia has  you know the the full-scale invasion is  only one of many different options right  right do you think putin has already  determined what he's going to do or a  lot of it will depend on how the u.s  responds whether the u.s sends troops to  nato whether nato speaks with the  unified voice i mean how much of this is  predetermined versus a  play-by-play  well this is the this is the big  question you know we're all we're all  trying to become uh mind readers these  days of of of putin trying to get inside  his head and work out where he's going  and what his plans are my personal  instinct is yes he has not fully decided  i think there's certainly a strong  element of intimidation going on here  that putin believes the west is weak uh  collectively he believes they will  ultimately fold so he's increasing the  pressure he's increasing the tensions  hoping that ultimately the west will  fold and give him uh sweeping  concessions  but that does not mean he is not  prepared to also go to the the active  military phase if need be i think  certainly he's looking at the moment to  see what the west will do in response  he's looking for signs of disunity he's  looking for signs of weakness  he's looking for readiness to compromise  now if he doesn't see any of that he's a  very strong united front from the west  it may give him cause to think twice  but it may not because i think the west  has been quite clear collectively  america and britain as well have been  quite clear that they will not send  troops to ukraine to fight with you you  know alongside ukrainians against russia  so the the  the the measures that putin is really  expecting to encounter are almost  exclusively finite of the financial  nature high sanctions  which might not be enough to deter it  russia has huge reserves built up  specifically to whether a major  sanctions storm yeah that pain might be  worth it for him peter ukraine is  dominating the headlines uh here in the  u.s it feels like last week was a real  inflection point and is now reportedly  one of the reasons why the u.s stock  market is plunging as well what does  washington and the rest of the u.s get  wrong misunderstand the most about the  situation on the ground in the ukraine  well i think that there's this there's a  lot of talk about the geopolitics  there's a lot of talk about putin about  what culture wants there's a lot of talk  about uh the actions of the west of nato  i think fundamentally if we if we look  at if we step back and look at this  whole crisis at the very heart of this  crisis we have the ukrainian people it  is their will and their agency here  that's driven this whole thing they are  the ones who sought to establish a  democracy  uh who sought to turn their country  towards euro atlantic integration to  join the eu  to establish a democratic system and  these are the reasons why putin has  has attacked the country beginning in  2014  because putin does not wish to see a  country so close historically to russia  but have a successful transition away  from authoritarianism to  uh european democracy and so he's very  very much um  committed to preventing ukraine from  making this transition he understands  that it's essentially it's an  existential threat to his own regime his  own authoritarian regime um and the  ukrainian people are very determined to  to continue with the path that they've  set out on and they won't change you  know if the west was for example were to  make some sort of a deal or try to make  a compromise with russia then the west  would then turn around and find the  ukrainian people would quite likely say  no sorry we're not going to accept this  ultimately fundamentally that's the  bottom line of this story the ukrainian  people are we should not underestimate  their agency  very important point you know i made a  mistake when i spoke just now calling it  the ukraine which is something that  people in the us do a lot and i think  that speaks to how um uneducated we are  about ukraine and culturally how  how close it is to russia i mean  vladimir putin has made the point that  ukraine and russia share very close  historical ties cultural ties even  from where you sit having lived in  ukraine for last for the last 20 years  does it look like that there on the  ground or has it changed a lot  well it certainly looked more like that  20 years ago than it does now  and i think that the ukraine is probably  the greatest of europe's uh historic  captive nations it was a as i mentioned  it's a huge country but it was  essentially dominated by foreign powers  primarily but not exclusively russia but  mostly russia for hundreds and hundreds  of years uh and during that period the  russians uh  regardless of whether it was the tsar  the czarist regime or then the soviet  regime afterwards they all shared uh  very strong uh policies of russification  um you know so the russian language  dominated russian culture dominated the  ukrainian ukrainian was reduced to the  level of it was  it was a kind of a dialect it was often  described as it was not you know you  have to speak russian to get on and uh  culturally historically they were always  promoted the idea that the countries  were you know ukraine was essentially a  a subgrouping of russia and so for many  many years this this went on and it's  only now over the last 30 years ukraine  is is making efforts to reclaim its  history so you shouldn't be too hard on  yourself for perhaps you know not  getting the correct challenge because  people do and people here do it  sometimes as well you know okay  because it's it's it's a process that's  very much ongoing and yeah it's part of  the reason we are where we are today  that ukraine's attempts to assert its  own uh identity uh put it in conflict  with russia you know russia regards kiev  it's important to remember russia  regards kiev as the mother city of  russia it's the home of it's the  birthplace of the russian nation so  uh  in a foreign country obviously  undermines russia's own claims and  ukrainians will say to russians well you  know actually  russia's roots are are to the east are  with the mongol empire  and the moscow's rise as a vassal state  of the mongol empire so  history is the history goes very very  deep in this and uh there are a lot of  intertwined uh moments in russian  ukrainian history but what we're seeing  now is essentially geopolitical divorce  and those divorces can be painful peter  i really appreciate your taking the time  to explain all that in the historical  context is fascinating peter dickinson  is ukraine editor at the atlantic  council he joins us from kiev

